cuems-common (1.3.0-21) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * NEW cuems-gpu-pin.service + /usr/lib/cuems/bin/cuems-gpu-pin: force the AMD
    GPU's DPM level to `high` at boot, on measured hardware only (869eq54nj).
    On the Maxtang FP530 (Picasso, 1002:15d8) sustained multi-4K decode
    collapses amdgpu's DPM to ~210-277 MHz after the first heavy rung per boot;
    only a reboot restores it, and newer GPU firmware does NOT fix it (tested
    2/2 on 20250410-2, hang signature identical). Pinning rescues the box at a
    measured +8 C (plateau 76.0 C at ~28 C ambient).
    SHIPS INERT. The helper exits 0 unless BOTH enabled=true in the new
    /etc/cuems/gpu-pin.conf (ships false) AND an allow-listed GPU is present,
    so this changes nothing on any existing host, exactly like the enabled=
    gate in cluster-poweroff.conf. The allow-list is one entry — the evidence
    is Picasso-only, and a future AMD SKU gets its own thermal soak first.
    The unit is enabled explicitly in postinst, not via WantedBy=: this
    package's postinst carries no #DEBHELPER# token, so dh_installsystemd
    generates nothing and a unit relying on WantedBy= alone would be installed
    but never symlinked, i.e. silently never run.
    Note for anyone writing a test against it: `high` is a CEILING, not a
    floor. The SMU still bounds the clock by the package power budget
    (550-985 MHz under load on this box, 1200 only in bursts), so any check of
    the form "sclk >= X while pinned" is wrong and fails on healthy hardware.
  * cuems-videocomposer.service: Environment=MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=4. Decode-thread
    churn warms up ~31 glibc secondary arenas (the 8 x nproc default) that
    glibc never unmaps — a bounded ~134 MB RSS plateau, not a leak. Measured
    across an interleaved n=3 matrix with decode occupancy flat at 57-58 %
    across all nine arms: -133.9 MB against within-treatment spreads of
    13-22 MB, drops indistinguishable from control. Cap 4 rather than 1 keeps
    3-4 arenas, so render and decode threads are not serialised through a
    single malloc lock; cap 1's extra 38 MB was judged not worth that risk at
    layer counts nobody has measured. Memory measure only — no effect on the
    hang boundary, on drops, or on VRAM.
  * cuems-hdmi-audio-map: detect the ALSA card instead of assuming Intel's
    name. The generator hardcoded hw:PCH, which is the card name on Intel
    hosts; on the AMD FP530 the HDMI card is `Generic`, so the generated
    /etc/asound.conf named a device that does not exist. jackd-cuems then
    failed to open it and crash-looped (235 restarts observed), taking
    cuems-node-engine down with it as a failed dependency — a video node dead
    at boot for an audio-routing reason. Now reads /proc/asound/card*/pcm*p/info
    for an HDMI-capable card and falls back to PCH.
  * videocomposer-flags.env.example: document the hang-guard operator flags
    (--hang-guard, --vc-profile, --saturation-monitor) shipped by
    cuems-videocomposer 0.1.2-6.

 -- Ion Reguera <ion@stagelab.coop>  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 18:34:00 +0200

cuems-common (1.3.0-20) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * interfaces.master: list ethernet0 before wifi0 in bond-slaves. A bond adopts
    the MAC of its FIRST slave and forces it onto the others, so listing wifi0
    first put the WiFi card's MAC on the ethernet wire — making a controller's
    DHCP identity depend on a chip it does not need. Measured 2026-08-31: on a
    kernel whose iwlwifi firmware was missing, wifi0 never appeared, the bond
    fell back to ethernet0's MAC, the reservation missed, and the host came up
    on a pool lease looking dead. The new order simply makes the adopted MAC
    agree with the bond-primary that was already declared; failover is decided
    by bond-primary + miimon and is unchanged, and the WiFi AP is unaffected
    because hostapd unenslaves wifi0 before binding. Controller-only: nodes use
    bridge0 and have no bond.
  * Retire /etc/network/interfaces and /etc/network/interfaces.cuems via
    rm_conffile in preinst/postinst/postrm. Versions old enough to ship them
    left dpkg recording both as `obsolete` conffiles on every host that came
    through the divert -> postinst-cp transition (verified on test2, 1.3.0-14).
    Two consequences this retires: `dpkg -S /etc/network/interfaces` still
    answered cuems-common, which reads as the package owning the live network
    config when it does not; and `dpkg --purge` would have DELETED the host's
    live /etc/network/interfaces, obsolete conffiles being removed on purge.
    The repo's two dead duplicates are deleted with them — the shipped template
    is usr/share/cuems/interfaces.master and always was.
  * CLAUDE.md: correct two false clauses in the same sentence. The templates ARE
    auto-deployed on first install (debian/postinst copies interfaces.master
    when [ -z "$2" ]), and nodeconf DOES touch network plumbing
    (CuemsNodeConf.py:770-777 copies the same template during controller
    promotion) — the note claimed the opposite "confirmed by source audit".
  * docs/node-identity-contract.md: say which MAC. The `mac` field is less
    stable than its table implies on controllers, `ethernet0`'s permanent
    address is what a DHCP reservation and WoL must key to, and the fleet's
    three coexisting states (unfixed / hwaddress-pinned / reordered) are named
    so an operator meeting a pinned box knows what they are looking at.

 -- Ion Reguera <ion@stagelab.coop>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 20:33:12 +0200

cuems-common (1.3.0-19) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * Retire /etc/default/grub.d/cuems-display.cfg, the last live remnant of the
    HDMI warm-restart hack. It appended `i915.enable_dc=0` to
    GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT; the hack was reverted in bd47669 (April 2026,
    the black screen was a monitor-side setup problem) and the file was dropped
    from debian/install — but with no rm_conffile, so dpkg marked it `obsolete`
    and kept it on disk forever.
  * Consequence, found on the FP530 on 2026-08-31: a fresh install has no such
    file, while every host upgraded across the revert still carries the
    parameter on EVERY boot entry, Debian's own included. Where an operator had
    also hand-added it to /etc/default/grub, it appears TWICE:
    `ro pci=nommconf i915.enable_dc=0 i915.enable_dc=0`. Nothing in any log says
    so, and the fleet silently splits into two populations depending on whether
    a box was installed before or after April.
  * Handled with dpkg-maintscript-helper rm_conffile across preinst/postinst/
    postrm, the pattern already used for the acpid files in 1.3.0-16 and for the
    same reason (debhelper cannot inject here — see the note atop postinst).
  * The postinst's update-grub then recompiles the menu without the parameter,
    since rm_conffile has already moved the file out of the *.cfg glob that
    grub-mkconfig sources.
  * NOT touched: a copy hand-added to /etc/default/grub itself. That is
    grub-common's conffile and host-level config; this package has no business
    rewriting it, so it stays an operator action.

