cuems-common (1.3.0-18) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * New /etc/grub.d/11_cuems_recovery: a GRUB boot entry that starts the box
    with cuems-videocomposer masked, so an operator in front of a broken video
    host always has a usable text console. The compositor takes DRM master
    over every connected output, which means the console disappears exactly
    when it is needed.
  * The mask is applied with systemd.mask= on the kernel command line, not on
    disk, so one ordinary reboot undoes it. quiet/splash are stripped so the
    boot is readable. Only the compositor is masked — this is a console escape
    hatch, not a "CUEMS off" switch.
  * Why it is in the package at all: the idea existed on ONE machine as a
    hand-placed /etc/grub.d/11_formitgo_recovery that no package owned
    (dpkg -S found nothing) and that no other host had. postinst removes that
    legacy file, so an upgraded FP530 does not end up with two entries.
  * One entry per each of the TWO newest kernels, newest first, with a stable
    --id (cuems-recovery-<kver>). The hand-written script always picked the
    newest kernel, so its recovery entry inherited whatever was wrong with the
    default boot — on the FP530 that is the backports 6.12.95, which comes up
    on the wrong IP. Capped at two because this package's own postinst
    disables apt-daily*.timer, so superseded kernels are never purged here.
  * Kernel/initrd paths come from grub-mkconfig_lib
    (make_system_path_relative_to_its_root + prepare_grub_to_access_device)
    instead of a hardcoded /boot/vmlinuz-<kver>, which silently assumes /boot
    is not a separate partition. A kernel with no matching initrd is skipped.
  * postinst and postrm both run update-grub, guarded so a host without GRUB
    still (de)configures cleanly — but with a WARNING on stderr rather than a
    bare `|| true`. A broken update-grub is host-wide: /etc/grub.d/* runs
    under set -e, so it also breaks the hook the kernel package runs.
  * postrm is not optional: files shipped under /etc/ become dpkg conffiles
    automatically, and neither remove nor purge regenerates grub.cfg. Without
    the hook the compiled menu would keep offering a recovery entry for a
    package that is gone.

 -- Ion Reguera <ion@stagelab.coop>  Sun, 31 Aug 2026 13:20:00 +0200

cuems-common (1.3.0-17) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * cluster-poweroff.conf: new nodes_off= knob (default true, so no site's
    behaviour changes). false powers the displays off and shuts the controller
    down but leaves the cluster nodes running.
  * Why it was needed: sala1 at Castillo Medina del Campo wants exactly that,
    and with no knob the only way to express it was to withhold the
    controller's SSH key from the node — encoding an operator decision as an
    authentication failure. That is invisible in config, reads as a fault in
    the journal ("Permission denied (publickey)"), silently reverts the day
    someone installs the key for good reasons, and costs the full node_wait_s
    on every power-off because a node that stays up is polled to the deadline
    (measured: +120 s on every power-button press).
  * Accepts the same true|yes|1|on vocabulary as enabled=, in both scripts.
  * Stage 2 now logs an explicit "SKIPPED — nodes_off=..." line, so a site that
    deliberately leaves its nodes up says so in the journal instead of looking
    like a broken SSH.

 -- Ion Reguera <ion@stagelab.coop>  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 14:13:44 +0200

cuems-common (1.3.0-16) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * Orderly cluster power-off on the physical power button, and confirmed
    projector power-on at boot. Both OFF by default: see enabled= in the new
    /etc/cuems/cluster-poweroff.conf. A fleet-wide upgrade therefore changes
    no site's behaviour until a box is opted in.
  * New cuems-cluster-poweroff.service: does nothing at boot, and on the way
    down its ExecStop= powers the projectors off (PJLink), then the cluster
    node(s) off over SSH, waits until they are really down, and hands back to
    systemd to power the controller off. The ordering is what makes it work:
    After=networking.service (ifupdown is what actually tears interfaces
    down; network-online.target is a passive boot synchronisation point and
    cannot carry a stop-direction guarantee) plus avahi-daemon.service AND
    .socket, so PJLink and <node>.local resolution still work when it runs;
    Before=cuems-power-bridge.service so the bridge has already stopped and
    its _cancel_projector_on_task() has run, ruling out a POWR 1 racing our
    POWR 0.
  * The hook duplicates no protocol logic: it drives the installed
    cuems-power-bridge library (PJLink driver, SSH fan-out, reachability
    poller) against this host's own power-bridge.conf, so ssh_user, ssh_key,
    poweroff_cmd, dry_run and projector_power_off_on_shutdown keep exactly
    their bridge meanings. dry_run=true gives a full-fidelity, zero-risk
    rehearsal of the whole sequence.
  * Guards, each earning its place: it acts only when a poweroff/halt job is
    actually in the transaction, so a reboot never kills the lamps (PJLink
    answers ERR3 to POWR 1 during cooldown, so a reboot that powered them off
    would return to a dark room) and `isolate rescue.target` or a stray
    `systemctl stop` are inert; self-exclusion by node uuid and by exact
    local-address membership, because a standalone controller is its own node
    and a host can never observe ITSELF unreachable, so a missed self-entry
    would burn the whole node_wait_s on every poweroff — and hostname
    matching alone would not catch it, since nodeconf never ran and the OS
    hostname is e.g. cupula1 while the avahi name is node01.local; and
    idempotency (query display state and node liveness before acting), which
    makes the second pass after a bridge-driven POST /shutdown a fast no-op
    instead of a redundant SSH fan-out and a POWR 0 into a cooldown window.
  * New cuems-displays-on.service: the bridge's projector_power_on_on_start
    fires one power-on whose retry budget is ~4s, while lamp cooldown is tens
    of seconds — so a fast power cycle left the room dark with a single
    unconfirmed WARNING. This re-issues POST /poweron until every display
    reports "on". Gated on projector_power_on_on_start as well as enabled=,
    so sites with real fleets deliberately left dark are never commanded.
  * RETIRED the dead acpid power-button chain:
    /etc/acpi/events/powerbtn-acpi-support, powerbtn-custom-handler.sh and
    cuems-power-button-waiter.sh (removed via a new
    debian/cuems-common.maintscript, since they are conffiles), plus
    /usr/bin/cuems-stop. acpid is not installed on any CUEMS host — verified
    on isil, test2 and both Medina controllers — so none of it has ever run;
    the button has always been handled by systemd-logind. Keeping it was a
    booby trap: cuems-stop is hardcoded to a long-gone venue's relay
    (192.168.3.20) and SSH targets (stagelab@192.168.2.20x), so installing
    acpid for any reason would have resurrected it. The `cuems-stop` shell
    ALIAS survives and is now unambiguous.
  * New etc/systemd/logind.conf.d/10-cuems-power-button.conf pins
    HandlePowerKey=poweroff. Behaviourally a no-op — it is already the
    compiled-in default — but the new hook depends on a press producing an
    ordinary orderly transaction, so the contract is now stated. Not
    reloadable (CanReload=no on systemd 252), so it applies at next boot;
    harmless, since it matches what is already in force.
  * cuems-hdmi-audio-map: stop asserting that JACK uses multi_hdmi. The
    generated asound.conf header said so unconditionally, which is FALSE on a
    venue whose sound goes to a house PA rather than the projectors -- cupula2
    runs jackd -dhw:PCH,0 (the ALC897 analog minijack) with every HDMI PCM
    closed, and that line would send anyone debugging audio to the wrong
    device. The header now reports the device actually configured in
    DAEMON_ARGS (/etc/default/jack, a conffile that is NOT regenerated at
    boot), flagging whether it is the minijack or the HDMI slots. Documentation
    only; no behavioural change.
  * Known blind spot, documented rather than papered over: `systemctl
    poweroff --force` skips the orderly per-unit stop, so no ExecStop runs
    and the projectors and nodes stay up.

 -- Ion Reguera <ion@stagelab.coop>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 12:29:07 +0200

cuems-common (1.3.0-15) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * journal maintenance: run on every host, and reclaim orphaned journal
    directories.
  * cuems-journal-vacuum.timer was WantedBy=cuems-controller.target, so
    on a node it could never fire. Nodes need it too: they inherit the
    same foreign machine-id journal directories from the master image,
    and a node cloned from a controller keeps a /var/log/journal/remote
    that nothing else would ever prune. Now WantedBy=timers.target, and
    the postinst enables it outside the controller-only branch. Also
    dropped PartOf=cuems-controller.target — a controller-target bounce
    must not cancel log maintenance.
  * cuems-journal-vacuum now also removes journal directories belonging
    to OTHER machines, inherited by cloning a master image. Nothing
    reclaimed them before: journald enforces SystemMaxUse= only inside
    its own machine-id directory and the old sweep covered remote/ only,
    so they were immortal — and journalctl --disk-usage does not count
    them, which is why the growth stayed invisible. Guarded: never this
    host's own directory, never remote/, the name must be machine-id
    shaped, and the directory must contain journal files and nothing
    else. Disable with CUEMS_JOURNAL_PRUNE_FOREIGN=0.
  * A missing /var/log/journal/remote no longer exits the script early,
    so the foreign-directory sweep still runs on hosts that never
    collected journals. ReadWritePaths widened to /var/log/journal
    accordingly.
  * Measured before the fix: 17-22G of /var/log on each of three fleet
    hosts (cupula2, isil controller, isil node01), ~11G of it orphaned
    directories on every one; ~45G reclaimed.

